1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Meet Respawn: a gaming marketplace concept built through Forge, using a live DeepSeek request and original GPT generated artwork.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Here is the complete flow.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
The process starts inside Forge.

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:22,000
I enter the local demo workspace, create a project, and describe the store: its audience, visual direction, products, and interactions.

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,000
The DeepSeek key stays on the local server.

6
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:32,000
In the project workspace, I select DeepSeek V4 Pro and generate the website.

7
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:37,000
This is the actual provider response, previewed inside Forge and exported as HTML.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
The ninety eight second generation wait has been removed.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The result is a standalone frontend.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The visual direction starts with original GPT artwork: a translucent controller, matching keyboard, headset, and mouse.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
Five compressed WebP assets keep the complete image set around one hundred sixty one kilobytes.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Now the exported store runs locally.

13
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
A demo login accepts a display name and updates the header.

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:10,000
This is an in memory identity for the walkthrough, with a working sign out action.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,000
The marketplace pairs a cinematic hero with a focused product catalog.

16
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:18,000
Each item has its own artwork, description, and price.

17
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Category controls narrow the selection to keyboards, audio, controllers, or mice.

18
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Sorting makes it easy to compare options by price before adding anything to the cart.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Search filters the catalog as you type.

20
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:37,000
An unmatched search gets a clear empty state and a reset action.

21
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:43,000
Heart controls save individual products, and the saved items view brings those choices together.

22
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
These interactions are working browser behavior, with no loading screens added for effect.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Add a controller and keyboard, then open the cart.

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The subtotal reflects both products.

25
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Increase a quantity, decrease it again, or remove an item; the total updates immediately.

26
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
During review, I fixed the generated cart handlers and improved dialog focus so these controls work reliably.

27
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Checkout shows the order summary and carries over the display name.

28
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:17,000
A required checkbox makes the demo boundary clear.

29
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Placing the order produces a confirmation and clears the cart.

30
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
No payment details are requested, no money is charged, and no real order is submitted.

31
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The storefront also has a light theme and a responsive layout.

32
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:39,000
I checked it at mobile, tablet, and desktop widths, along with keyboard focus, reduced motion, and zoom.

33
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Forge's verification suite passed too.

34
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
The original provider output is preserved separately from the reviewed and refined website.

35
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
The finished package includes the local website, portable HTML, original artwork, and this three minute walkthrough.

36
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Respawn is a working frontend concept, ready for the next stage: connecting real services.
